<h2>The legal frame: CPC in the Family Court</h2>
<p>Section 10 of the Family Courts Act, 1984 applies the Code of Civil Procedure to Family Court proceedings, subject to the Act and any rules, and leaves the court free to lay down its own procedure to arrive at a settlement or the truth. Order VI Rule 17 CPC therefore governs amendments: the court may <em>at any stage</em> allow either party to alter or amend pleadings in such manner and on such terms as may be just, and <em>all</em> amendments shall be made as are necessary for determining the real questions in controversy. The proviso adds the discipline: after trial commences, no amendment unless due diligence is shown.</p>

<h2>Why matrimonial cases amend more than others</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Grounds that grow</h4><p>Cruelty and desertion are not single events but continuing conduct. Each fresh incident is both new evidence and, if pleaded, new material for the ground itself — and unpleaded incidents cannot ordinarily be proved.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Litigation as conduct</h4><p>What happens inside the litigation — complaints filed during its pendency, allegations made in pleadings, conduct at mediation — can itself constitute cruelty, and comes into existence only after filing.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Changing reliefs</h4><p>Custody arrangements, maintenance needs and residence circumstances shift over long proceedings; prayers may need updating so the decree matches the family\'s present reality.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Subsequent events: the companion doctrine</h2>
<p>Courts have long recognised that in continuing relationships they may take note of events after the institution of proceedings — to shorten litigation, to do complete justice between the parties, and to mould the relief to the situation that actually exists at the decree. In matrimonial practice this surfaces in familiar ways: the completion of the desertion period during the case, reconciliation and its breakdown, the birth of a child, a party\'s remarriage-adjacent conduct, or compliance and defiance of interim orders. The safe course is always to bring such events on record by a formal amendment or additional pleading rather than springing them in evidence or arguments — a fact not pleaded is a fact the other side never had to meet, and decrees have been upset for less.</p>

<h2>How courts decide amendment applications</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weighs in favour</th><th>Weighs against</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Necessity</td><td>Amendment needed to decide the real controversy between the spouses</td><td>Material peripheral to any pleaded ground</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stage</td><td>Before issues or early in evidence; genuinely new events at any stage</td><td>Post-trial amendment of facts known all along</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Due diligence</td><td>Events after filing; facts discovered later despite diligence</td><td>Facts within the party\'s knowledge, omitted without explanation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prejudice</td><td>The other side can answer by reply and evidence; costs can compensate</td><td>Withdrawal of admissions; a wholly new, inconsistent case</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bona fides</td><td>Prompt application once the event occurs</td><td>Amendment sought to stall an imminent decree</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice pointers for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For the petitioner:</strong> maintain a running chronology after filing; move amendment promptly after each significant event rather than hoarding incidents for one late application that invites the due-diligence objection.</li>
    <li><strong>For the respondent:</strong> an amendment allowed is not an allegation proved — insist on the right to file an amended written statement, seek recall of witnesses where evidence has begun, and ask for costs where the amendment is late.</li>
    <li><strong>For both:</strong> amendments in the divorce petition often ripple into connected proceedings — maintenance, custody, DV applications — and consistency across pleadings is scrutinised in cross-examination.</li>
    <li><strong>Drafting:</strong> set out each new event as a separately numbered paragraph with date, place and witnesses, and amend the prayer where the relief changes; vague "continuing cruelty" paragraphs add little.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The limits</h2>
<p>Amendment cannot resurrect what the statute bars: it cannot manufacture a cause of action that did not exist at filing where the law requires it then (a desertion ground filed before the two-year period is complete is the standard example — though the period completing during a long trial has been treated pragmatically where justice demands), cannot withdraw clear admissions, and cannot convert the petition into a different species of proceeding altogether. Within those limits, the jurisdiction is generous — because in matrimonial litigation, more than anywhere else, the dispute the court decides should be the dispute the parties are actually living.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article sets out procedural law in general terms and is not legal advice. The fate of an amendment application always depends on the stage and facts of the particular case.</p>
</div>
